WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.400
There’s a pretty awesome scene in The Fate
of the Furious

00:00:02.400 --> 00:00:05.439
where Jason Statham fights a bunch of armed
mercenaries on an airplane

00:00:05.439 --> 00:00:08.210
while holding an adorable baby in a carrier.

00:00:08.210 --> 00:00:11.140
I've always wondered how babies end up in
situations like this.

00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:13.330
What kind of hoops do you have to jump through
to cast babies in Hollywood?

00:00:13.330 --> 00:00:15.220
You were gonna shoot a baby?

00:00:15.220 --> 00:00:17.820
You sick bastard.

00:00:17.820 --> 00:00:23.180
Besides babysitting, delivering newspapers,
and, uh, wreath making,

00:00:23.180 --> 00:00:25.210
show business is the only industry that can
legally

00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:27.540
employ children under the age of 14

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:31.230
In fact, they’ve been in movies almost since
the birth of cinema.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:33.730
I hate myself so much.

00:00:33.730 --> 00:00:36.489
Casting a baby isn’t as simple as casting
a grown up actor though,

00:00:36.489 --> 00:00:37.699
For one: they don’t have to get their shift
at

00:00:37.699 --> 00:00:39.600
Starbucks covered to go to an audition,

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.140
but mostly because there are some special
rules

00:00:41.140 --> 00:00:43.609
producers have to follow when casting an infant.

00:00:43.609 --> 00:00:45.649
For example, in California, babies can only
be

00:00:45.649 --> 00:00:48.289
on set for two consecutive hours per day,

00:00:48.289 --> 00:00:52.329
with actual work not exceeding 20 minutes
“under any circumstances.”

00:00:52.329 --> 00:00:54.850
The state’s child labor laws also require
that babies

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:58.030
be at least 15 days old to be eligible for
a work permit.

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:01.280
Which is good news for babies who can
just chill during their literal first days

00:01:01.280 --> 00:01:02.499
on earth,

00:01:02.499 --> 00:01:05.019
but not so good for productions that have
scenes with newborns

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:09.159
because the thing is, if you’ve ever seen
a baby that’s two weeks old,

00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:12.200
you know that they don’t exactly look like
a newborn anymore.

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:15.970
In order to get past this hurdle, productions
often try to cast preemies,

00:01:15.970 --> 00:01:19.920
who look newborn even when they’re the required
minimum of 15 days old,

00:01:19.920 --> 00:01:23.520
and in a lot of places outside of California
that’s perfectly legal.

00:01:23.520 --> 00:01:27.969
In 16 states there are actually zero regulations
regarding minimum age for baby employment

00:01:27.969 --> 00:01:28.969
in films

00:01:28.969 --> 00:01:32.320
and many other states have laws that are pretty
lax.

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:37.009
In New Jersey for example a month-old baby
can work five hours a day, five days a week

00:01:37.009 --> 00:01:40.689
and in Louisiana they can be employed six
hours a day, six days a week.

00:01:40.689 --> 00:01:44.710
That’s more hours than I worked when I
bagged groceries after I graduated college

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:48.460
and no I DO NOT want to talk about it.

00:01:48.460 --> 00:01:51.439
Most states also do nothing to protect the
money that babies make.

00:01:51.439 --> 00:01:53.369
Only a few places follow the Coogan Law,

00:01:53.369 --> 00:01:55.149
which requires that child actors be set up
with a

00:01:55.149 --> 00:01:57.939
blocked trust account to protect their earnings.

00:01:57.939 --> 00:01:59.780
The law is named after Jackie Coogan,

00:01:59.780 --> 00:02:01.861
an early 20th century child actor who was
in

00:02:01.861 --> 00:02:03.799
a number of Charlie Chaplin films

00:02:03.799 --> 00:02:06.450
including the American classic The Kid.

00:02:06.450 --> 00:02:09.740
As a child actor he reportedly earned as much
as $4 million,

00:02:09.740 --> 00:02:13.530
but his parents squandered all his money
by the time he became an adult.

00:02:13.530 --> 00:02:15.910
Because of his s***ty parents, moms and dads
in these states

00:02:15.910 --> 00:02:19.940
are required to set aside 15 persent of their
minor’s income in a Coogan account,

00:02:19.940 --> 00:02:22.750
which the child can access at the age of 18.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:25.940
Shooting with babies can be complicated,
but the film industry has a way of working

00:02:25.940 --> 00:02:26.940
around it.

00:02:26.940 --> 00:02:32.920
I would say 90 to 95 percent of jobs for babies
and toddlers

00:02:32.920 --> 00:02:36.310
are exclusive to twins and triplets

00:02:36.310 --> 00:02:39.660
because two babies can play one character.

00:02:39.660 --> 00:02:41.410
So there's two advantages there.

00:02:41.410 --> 00:02:44.780
Number one you can have them both come to
set at the same time.

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.560
So if Twin A gets fussy they can call in Twin
B

00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:49.860
and they can use the babies back and forth.

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:52.860
And the other thing is sometimes production
will split their call times

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:56.020
so they'll have one baby come in let's say
at 9:00 in the morning

00:02:56.020 --> 00:02:59.030
have another baby come in at 2:00 in the afternoon.

00:02:59.030 --> 00:03:01.150
And so it extends their day.

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:05.010
Also half of all twins and almost all triplets
are born premature,

00:03:05.010 --> 00:03:08.410
so productions get more working hours and
they look newborn.

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:10.530
Sean Penn had a different solution.

00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:13.190
He directed a movie that nobody saw called
The Last Face

00:03:13.190 --> 00:03:16.670
where they put out a casting call for a one
day old baby.

00:03:16.670 --> 00:03:19.660
Since they were shooting in South Africa,
where you can cast minors with the permission

00:03:19.660 --> 00:03:21.110
of the Department of Labour,

00:03:21.110 --> 00:03:23.160
it was totally legal.

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:26.400
Babies in show biz probably rake in big bucks
right?

00:03:26.400 --> 00:03:28.530
Unfortunately… not.

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.000
Babies obviously can’t talk,

00:03:30.000 --> 00:03:32.370
so they are usually classified as an “under
5”,

00:03:32.370 --> 00:03:34.630
a term that refers to an actor with less than
five lines.

00:03:34.630 --> 00:03:40.650
It's interesting because you can have a baby
who is a recurring character on a TV show

00:03:40.650 --> 00:03:43.660
and has a name that the public can relate
to.

00:03:43.660 --> 00:03:47.050
But a lot of those babies are only getting
paid

00:03:47.050 --> 00:03:50.800
between 200 and 300 dollars a day.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:52.960
So babies don’t really make a ton of money.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:54.780
And casting them sounds like kind of a nightmare
--

00:03:54.780 --> 00:03:57.300
are there any other options?

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.840
Cut to American Sniper:

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.590
Not all of them are as fake as this one,

00:04:01.590 --> 00:04:04.320
but plenty of productions resort to using
fake babies,

00:04:04.320 --> 00:04:06.190
at least for part of the shoot.

00:04:06.190 --> 00:04:07.190
Hey!

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:08.550
Tend to my daughter please, right now!

00:04:08.550 --> 00:04:11.651
It is quite common that you know they’ll
use fake babies for

00:04:11.651 --> 00:04:14.480
the far away scenes and then you know they'll
hire

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.400
a baby when they need a close up shot or a
face shot.

00:04:17.400 --> 00:04:20.280
And there are robot babies out there that
look pretty convincing.

00:04:20.280 --> 00:04:22.440
And by convincing I mean terrifying.

00:04:22.440 --> 00:04:26.660
I never thought I’d use a Mandy Moore clip
in one of my videos but here we go:

00:04:26.660 --> 00:04:28.870
They have this one robotic baby.

00:04:28.870 --> 00:04:30.231
It’s weird guys.

00:04:30.231 --> 00:04:33.930
I have two very emotional scenes where I’m
talking to

00:04:33.930 --> 00:04:35.540
a newborn in the hospital

00:04:35.540 --> 00:04:38.010
and I go to put my hand on the robotic baby

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:42.520
and it’s like vibrating and like making
these weird movements.

00:04:42.520 --> 00:04:45.460
Babies on screen aren’t going away anytime
soon.

00:04:45.460 --> 00:04:49.040
So until producers find a cheap and easy CGI
fix for the baby dilemma,

00:04:49.040 --> 00:04:50.721
it looks like they’ll continue to have to
work

00:04:50.721 --> 00:04:53.060
within the regulations that are set up.

00:04:53.060 --> 00:04:56.220
Or they'll use demonic robot infants who will
take over humanity.

